WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:10.840
One of the,

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:14.160
junior nurses said to me,
I used to have a pocket

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:17.400
full of papers, and I used to have to,
before the end of my shift,

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.280
I’d look at all my bits of paper
to make sure I've done everything.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.280
Now, with the Federated Data
Platform, it's all in the system,

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:25.040
so she doesn't have to worry about it.

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:28.320
And she says it makes a difference
because she would quite often go home,

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:33.040
sit in the car, in the car park and think,
oh gosh, I've forgotten about Mrs.

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:36.360
or whatever, and she would run back
in or she’d phone somebody

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:40.440
that again shows you the level of care
that our staff have

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.080
for their patients.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.200
A lot of clinicians in their day to day
roles, when you speak to them,

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.120
they're logging into
7, 8 different systems

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:55.240
just to complete
one patient transfer

00:00:55.240 --> 00:01:00.840
Spreadsheets, bits of paper, email trails,
you know, the logistics of care.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:04.400
That’s how I think about it
has many steps and many handovers.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:09.000
There are a number of logistics activities
that need to happen and come together

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.120
in order for you to be released
from the hospital.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:14.400
You've got all this data,

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.640
all in different shapes,
and we want to bring it together

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:18.960
so that you can see everything
in one view.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.600
That basically means we're connecting
and liberating data

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.720
from siloed systems
into a single repository.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:27.480
Now the opportunity on the
back of that is phenomenal.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:36.120
So the NHS Federated Data Platform
is software

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:41.480
that enables us to connect together
lots of our local organisational systems,

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:47.240
the asset management system, your
workforce system, your patient system.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:52.160
It allows us to connect all those together
so that we can use that information

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:53.760
in operational workflows.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:58.360
What we designed in the FDP was we created

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:03.400
segregated instances, which every NHS
organisation owns for themselves,

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:07.760
but underpinned by what we refer to
as the Canonical Data Model, the CDM.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:12.920
The CDM is consistent across
every instance that we deploy.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:16.400
Data should not have to leave
a sovereign organisation.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.360
It can be called and queried upon

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:24.000
need rather than it needs to move
every single time.

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:28.480
Foundry as a tool that underpins
the Federated Data Platform, provides

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:31.480
this unique capability of
Purpose-based Access Controls.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:33.400
So the data that you have

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:36.400
is only for the use
that it's already been permitted for.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:40.120
This is from my perspective,
is pretty unique to the Foundry tool.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:43.440
And I've been absolutely blown away
by this capability.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:47.920
So we spend a lot of time on-site, meeting
with real operational teams, frontline

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.440
clinicians, and trying to experience
those problems firsthand.

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:56.040
Working Palantir is fantastic
because you've got very young, dedicated

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:58.920
kind of

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:01.880
guys that just want to get on
with doing things.

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:04.960
They are very happy to have a go
and iterate.

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:05.880
Largely for me,

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:08.040
Palantir has been really good in that,
they've kind of

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:10.400
given me a lot of responsibility
and a lot of autonomy

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:13.040
and put me on a problem actually,
I cared quite a lot about.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:15.960
It's been fantastic
working with Palantir as a partner.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:19.600
We are at the table together,
working with our local organisations,

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:24.040
thinking about innovative solutions
to the biggest challenges

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:29.000
that the NHS is facing today
and the tenacity,

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.800
the skills that the Palantir engineers
bring is phenomenal.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:39.320
We were able to build

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:43.680
the first Theatre Scheduling product
in a matter of weeks. 

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:50.480
From ideation to and concept to product was done
I think in about three weeks.

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:53.000
And we were testing

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:56.920
with one clinician at the hospital
and a theatre manager.

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:00.840
It was demonstrating activities
which previously took over

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:04.200
four minutes to do,
to be done in 30 seconds.

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:08.960
We were able to look at individual
cases of patients who had had appointments

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:10.320
cancelled at one trust,

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:11.560
and they've been transferred over

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:14.040
to another trust
and were able to be seen sooner.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:17.040
I think on average, people
being seen about 40 days sooner,

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:21.200
some up to two, three months sooner,
which is incredible.

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:24.400
And this is people
just going 20 minutes down the road

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:26.920
to a different hospital
who has more capacity.

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:29.680
Every hospital
that uses our Inpatient product

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:33.600
generally sees over 120 extra procedures

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.600
per month, per trust that uses it.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:39.800
We've already delivered over 80,000

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:42.680
extra procedures
since the rollout of FDP.

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:47.760
That's 80,000 extra patients
been seen in existing NHS capacity.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:53.160
What's been brilliant

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:56.320
is hearing the feedback
from our local organisations.

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:00.400
So we have maybe anaesthetist
in a hospital that say it's

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:04.560
normally really difficult for me
to understand what my schedule looks like.

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:07.480
And actually with the FDP
I can log in myself.

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:11.840
I know my patient list and “game
changing” is one of the phrases

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:13.040
that I hear the most.

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:15.480
One of our matron
said, you've changed my life.

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:16.720
It's just so much easier.

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:18.320
I can log into one system,

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:22.440
I can access the information I need,
and I can discharge my patients on time.

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:24.960
People like me do this job for a reason,
right?

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:27.600
It's for public value.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:32.200
And sometimes you come across things
that really melt your heart.

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:34.480
It really gets to you.

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:38.160
The consultant, cancer consultant

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:40.680
 that we were working
on, on the Cancer Waiting List tool.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:45.400
What she said to me was,
since using the Federated Data Platform,

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:50.200
she's been able to space her day out
in a way that means that she can spread

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:54.120
good news amongst the bad news
that she has to give to patients.

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:57.720
Now, that seems quite a small thing,

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:02.520
but if you're giving bad news
all the time, it really means a lot.

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:06.120
And so to me, the FDP provides
an opportunity

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:10.680
for us to actually systematically solve
some of those bigger problems

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:11.320
in a way

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:15.000
that isn't just limited to a trust,
but it's actually kind of like scalable

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:19.280
and will actually make a larger difference
across the whole NHS.

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:25.240
The skills that the Palantir engineers bring to
the NHS FDP programme are phenomenal.

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:29.960
Their technical capabilities,
their engineering capabilities,

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:34.440
their business acumen and their ability
to work with our local organisations

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:37.640
and really understand
what those challenges are, is fantastic.

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:41.200
And that means that when we're presented
with the biggest challenges

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:42.960
that the NHS faces today,

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:46.560
having a Palantir engineer working side
by side with those in partnership

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:50.560
means that we know
that we'll come up with a solution

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:54.880
that means that we can deliver
better patient outcomes and services.

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:57.960
So we have a lot of staff that burn out

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:02.400
because of the extraordinary, tasks
that they have to do

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:05.680
and the amount
of bureaucracy in the system.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:08.800
So being able to help deliver

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:12.320
a tool like the Federated
Data Platform is immensely helpful.

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:15.360
And for me, that's kind of
worth all its weight in gold.

